SSOD SYSTEM FINAL IMPLEMENTATION GUIDE
\f1\b0 \
The Sigma-Sync Orbital Drive (SSOD) is a dynamic drivetrain system designed to manage vehicle mass inertia. It swaps driving axles on the fly without torque interruption by accelerating the airborne axle to the exact road speed (
\f0\b e-Sync
\f1\b0 ) and recovering the kinetic energy of the rising axle into electrical storage (
\f0\b KERS
\f1\b0 ).\
\
\
\
\
\
1. MECHANICAL ARCHITECTURE AND MATERIALS\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Sigma Axle (Pusher):
\f1\b0  Positioned in front of the main drive axle. The chassis is reinforced with 10 mm S700MC high-strength steel.\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Mechanical Interlock (Rocker Arm):
\f1\b0  Manufactured from 42CrMo4 quenched and tempered steel. The H-profile rocker arm ensures physical locking: when one axle rises, the lever mechanically forces the other down.\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Wheelbase:
\f1\b0  A 1350 mm clearance ensures an unobstructed 250 mm vertical travel range.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
